// nes/blue-bioeconomy.js
try { (() => {
/* Auto-generated NES text wrapper so the maker loads it via <script> (works even when the file is opened directly, no fetch/CORS). Edit the .md and regenerate, or edit here. */
window.CX_NES_TEXT = window.CX_NES_TEXT || {};
window.CX_NES_TEXT["blue"] = "# National Export Strategy — Blue Bioeconomy (Sector Summary)\n\n## Vision\nPosition Curaçao as a Caribbean hotspot for biodiversity-based business: a place\nwhere the island's exceptional marine environment is translated into research,\nproducts and investment, sustainably and at scale.\n\n## Why now\nThe global marine-biotechnology market is projected to grow from roughly USD 4.7\nbillion to USD 7.4 billion through 2026. Curaçao enters this window with a natural\nand institutional head start that very few small island states can match.\n\n## Curaçao's position\n- The island is fringed by approximately 103 km² of coral reef, among the best\n  studied in the Caribbean, with new species described from Curaçao waters.\n- The reef is not only an ecological asset but an economic one: it is estimated to\n  generate over USD 445 million a year in value today.\n- Curaçao's reef has already contributed to global medicine: the anti-cancer\n  compound Curacin A and the sea-sponge chemistry behind Remdesivir trace back to\n  organisms found here.\n- Deep, clear water close to shore enables a Deep Sea Water Industry that can cut\n  building-cooling energy use by up to 90%.\n\n## Key institutions and companies\n- **CARMABI** — research institute and park manager. Hosts roughly 250 scientists a\n  year from 62 institutions across 21 countries. Deep local expertise and strong\n  visual access to the reef.\n- **Curaçao Sea Aquarium** — marine research facility operating the research vessel\n  Chapman and the Curasub research submarine.\n- **Blue Halo Curaçao / Waitt Institute** — flagship marine spatial-planning\n  initiative; the policy-to-practice backbone for sustainable use.\n- **SECORE International** — coral reproduction and reef restoration; the annual\n  coral-spawning events are a rare scientific and visual moment.\n- **University of Curaçao (UoC)** — ocean-science teaching and the local talent\n  pipeline that makes the sector investable.\n- **PROTEUS (Fabien Cousteau Ocean Learning Center)** — an ambitious future\n  underwater research habitat. Concept/planned stage.\n\n## The active work\nBioprospecting moves along a value chain from discovery in the reef, to laboratory\ndevelopment, toward product. Research vessels and a submarine give scientists direct\naccess to depth. Restoration programmes rebuild reef while the science continues.\n\n## Investment opportunities\n- Marine-biotech research and product development anchored to CARMABI's pipeline.\n- Deep Sea Water cooling infrastructure for buildings and districts.\n- Restoration-linked and reef-positive tourism and education ventures.\n- Talent and laboratory capacity expansion through UoC partnerships.\n\n## CINEX's role\nCINEX facilitates: connecting investors, research institutions and partners to the\nsector, and helping turn Curaçao's living marine asset into lasting opportunity for\nyu di Kòrsou. CINEX is not the operator of these projects; it is the connector.\n";
})(); } catch (e) { __ds_ns.__errors.push({ path: "nes/blue-bioeconomy.js", error: String((e && e.message) || e) }); }

// nes/port-maritime-services.js
try { (() => {
/* Auto-generated NES text wrapper so the maker loads it via <script> (works even when the file is opened directly, no fetch/CORS). Edit the .md and regenerate, or edit here. */
window.CX_NES_TEXT = window.CX_NES_TEXT || {};
window.CX_NES_TEXT["port"] = "# National Export Strategy — Port & Maritime Services (Sector Summary)\n\n## Vision\nMake Curaçao the Caribbean specialist in value-added maritime support services. The\ngoal is not to be the largest port, but the best at high-value repair, refit and\nsupport work, anchored by world-class facilities and a trusted flag.\n\n## Curaçao's position\n- The **Port of Willemstad** is the second-largest seaport of the Netherlands.\n- Curaçao sits outside the main hurricane belt, giving naturally sheltered harbours.\n  Ships pay for that structural safety advantage, especially during hurricane season.\n- The island offers a full chain of services: repair, refit, bunkering, crew\n  training and ship registration.\n\n## Why now\nSuperyachts already made up around 34% of visiting vessels, and hurricane-season\nstorage and refit for that segment is still underexploited. There is also a clear\nopening in green vessel recycling on the CDM Holding terrain. The demand for safe,\nhigh-value maritime services in the region is growing.\n\n## Key institutions and companies\n- **Damen Shiprepair Curaçao (DSC)** — the largest shipyard in the Caribbean and the\n  Damen Group's only yard on the American continent. The anchor proof of the sector,\n  with dramatic visuals.\n- **Curaçao Ports Authority (CPA)** — manages the ports and operations such as KTK\n  Tugs and CPO. Institutional credibility and the big-picture data.\n- **Maritime Authority of Curaçao (MAC)** — runs the Curaçao Ship Register, offering\n  a Kingdom of the Netherlands flag and European access.\n- **Curoil N.V.** — bunkering and fuel logistics, operating around the clock at the\n  mega pier.\n- **De Ruyter Training & Consultancy (DRTC)** — maritime training school whose\n  instructors and students export skills across the region.\n- **Curaçao Marine** — a 25,000 m² yard with 140+ dry berths, serving the yachting\n  and marina subsector.\n\n## The active work\nThe shipyard runs continuous repair and refit. Bunkering operates around the clock.\nTraining schools certify crew that work across the Caribbean. The ship register\ngrows the fleet sailing under the Curaçao flag.\n\n## Investment opportunities\n- Superyacht hurricane-season storage, refit and service capacity.\n- Green ship recycling and circular-maritime operations.\n- Expansion of value-added repair, components and support services.\n- Maritime training capacity and certification, exported regionally.\n\n## CINEX's role\nCINEX facilitates: connecting investors and operators to Curaçao's maritime cluster\nand helping the island become the region's specialist in value-added maritime\nservices. CINEX is the connector, not the operator.\n";
})(); } catch (e) { __ds_ns.__errors.push({ path: "nes/port-maritime-services.js", error: String((e && e.message) || e) }); }

